WEBVTT

NOTE Sentence/phrase timings estimated from the matching narration segment, not word-aligned transcription.

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.326 line:76% position:50% size:90%
This is ValueLens, a guide to Copilot
adoption and estimated value.

2
00:00:04.326 --> 00:00:07.344 line:76% position:50% size:90%
We will go through every view in the
browser app.

3
00:00:07.344 --> 00:00:09.253 line:76% position:50% size:90%
For each one, we will ask three

4
00:00:09.253 --> 00:00:11.039 line:76% position:50% size:90%
questions. What does it show?

5
00:00:11.039 --> 00:00:12.455 line:76% position:50% size:90%
How should you read it?

6
00:00:12.455 --> 00:00:14.180 line:76% position:50% size:90%
And what should you do next?

7
00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:16.274 line:76% position:50% size:90%
This is not an installation video.

8
00:00:16.274 --> 00:00:20.400 line:76% position:50% size:90%
It is a practical guide to using the pages
once your data is ready.

9
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.202 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Every app screen in this guide uses the
public synthetic sample.

10
00:00:25.202 --> 00:00:27.891 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The people, agents and results are made
up.

11
00:00:27.891 --> 00:00:29.955 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The narration is generated by AI.

12
00:00:29.955 --> 00:00:32.581 line:76% position:50% size:90%
We are not looking at a live organisation.

13
00:00:32.581 --> 00:00:36.583 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Worked examples are also fictional, and
are labelled separately.

14
00:00:36.583 --> 00:00:39.522 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The sample is useful for learning the
controls.

15
00:00:39.522 --> 00:00:42.524 line:76% position:50% size:90%
It cannot show whether your own data
collection,

16
00:00:42.524 --> 00:00:45.150 line:76% position:50% size:90%
identity mapping or licences are complete.

17
00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:48.600 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Keep three kinds of information apart.

18
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.923 line:76% position:50% size:90%
First, recorded activity, such as sessions
in an audit export.

19
00:00:52.923 --> 00:00:57.874 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Second, labels assigned by rules, such as
a habit stage or a fit grade.

20
00:00:57.874 --> 00:01:02.406 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Third, estimates, such as equivalent hours
and their money value.

21
00:01:02.406 --> 00:01:06.172 line:76% position:50% size:90%
More activity does not automatically mean
better work.

22
00:01:06.172 --> 00:01:11.750 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A fit flag is not an answer-quality score,
and modelled value is not cash saved.

23
00:01:12.617 --> 00:01:15.439 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The header shows the date range of the
loaded

24
00:01:15.439 --> 00:01:18.261 line:76% position:50% size:90%
data and whether the app is using its
sample.

25
00:01:18.261 --> 00:01:22.025 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The tabs group the report into seven data
areas and a Guide.

26
00:01:22.025 --> 00:01:24.345 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Some tabs have a second row of views.

27
00:01:24.345 --> 00:01:26.791 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Start by checking the source and dates.

28
00:01:26.791 --> 00:01:30.241 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Then choose an organisation or another
relevant filter.

29
00:01:30.241 --> 00:01:34.067 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Compare like with like, using the same
period and population.

30
00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:39.226 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Different pages answer different
questions, so their filters differ.

31
00:01:39.226 --> 00:01:42.176 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Activation starts with dates and
organisation.

32
00:01:42.176 --> 00:01:45.189 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Other views add licence, tool or agent
filters.

33
00:01:45.189 --> 00:01:49.164 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A filter that does not apply is cleared
when you change views.

34
00:01:49.164 --> 00:01:53.459 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Do not assume that a filter from the
previous page is still active.

35
00:01:53.459 --> 00:01:55.767 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Read the visible controls each time.

36
00:01:55.767 --> 00:01:58.717 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Use Clear filters to return to the broad
view.

37
00:01:59.533 --> 00:02:03.913 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Change data loads processed interactions,
not the raw audit export.

38
00:02:03.913 --> 00:02:07.835 line:76% position:50% size:90%
An organisation and licence file improves
population counts.

39
00:02:07.835 --> 00:02:11.169 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Registry and feedback files add their own
evidence.

40
00:02:11.169 --> 00:02:14.829 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The app reads and calculates these files
in the browser.

41
00:02:14.829 --> 00:02:17.902 line:76% position:50% size:90%
That does not make filters a security
boundary:

42
00:02:17.902 --> 00:02:20.909 line:76% position:50% size:90%
anyone with a supplied file can read its
rows.

43
00:02:20.909 --> 00:02:24.112 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Share files only with people allowed to
see them.

44
00:02:24.112 --> 00:02:26.923 line:76% position:50% size:90%
ValueLens is experimental and audit
figures

45
00:02:26.923 --> 00:02:29.733 line:76% position:50% size:90%
can differ from official Microsoft
reports.

46
00:02:30.533 --> 00:02:32.517 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Open Assumptions in the header.

47
00:02:32.517 --> 00:02:35.078 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The hourly value prices a person’s time.

48
00:02:35.078 --> 00:02:37.766 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The licence price is per person per month.

49
00:02:37.766 --> 00:02:42.822 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The effort scenario selects conservative,
typical or optimistic time estimates.

50
00:02:42.822 --> 00:02:45.831 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The time-saved penalty reduces the money
value,

51
00:02:45.831 --> 00:02:48.519 line:76% position:50% size:90%
and work weight grading changes fit rules.

52
00:02:48.519 --> 00:02:52.359 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Settings apply immediately across the app
and are not saved.

53
00:02:52.359 --> 00:02:54.983 line:76% position:50% size:90%
They do not change the recorded activity.

54
00:02:55.783 --> 00:03:00.990 line:76% position:50% size:90%
In this source, estimated money value is
equivalent hours, multiplied by hourly

55
00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:03.165 line:76% position:50% size:90%
value, multiplied by the penalty.

56
00:03:03.165 --> 00:03:07.515 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The displayed hours themselves are not
multiplied by that penalty.

57
00:03:07.515 --> 00:03:12.722 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The currency setting changes the symbol;
it is not an exchange-rate conversion.

58
00:03:12.722 --> 00:03:17.731 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Record the assumptions alongside any
exported finding, so another person can

59
00:03:17.731 --> 00:03:21.883 line:76% position:50% size:90%
understand why the same activity produced
a different estimate.

60
00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:26.462 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Here is a fictional example, not a result
from the dashboard.

61
00:03:26.462 --> 00:03:29.360 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Twenty equivalent hours at fifty pounds an
hour

62
00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.012 line:76% position:50% size:90%
gives one thousand pounds before a
penalty.

63
00:03:32.012 --> 00:03:36.144 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A penalty of one half gives five hundred
pounds of estimated value.

64
00:03:36.144 --> 00:03:37.624 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The hours remain twenty.

65
00:03:37.624 --> 00:03:39.845 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Changing the hourly rate changes the

66
00:03:39.845 --> 00:03:42.496 line:76% position:50% size:90%
valuation, not the amount of work
observed.

67
00:03:42.496 --> 00:03:45.950 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Use a cautious scenario when presenting a
business case.

68
00:03:46.783 --> 00:03:51.599 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Activation compares recorded active users
with the organisation roster.

69
00:03:51.599 --> 00:03:57.160 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The two-sided chart puts inactive users on
the left and active users on the right.

70
00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:00.416 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The population buttons switch between all
users,

71
00:04:00.416 --> 00:04:03.739 line:76% position:50% size:90%
licensed users, unlicensed users and agent
users.

72
00:04:03.739 --> 00:04:05.977 line:76% position:50% size:90%
These are different denominators.

73
00:04:05.977 --> 00:04:11.267 line:76% position:50% size:90%
An active user in the core measure has a
prompt or Cowork usage row; it is not

74
00:04:11.267 --> 00:04:14.183 line:76% position:50% size:90%
simply anyone whose name appears in a
file.

75
00:04:15.033 --> 00:04:20.207 line:76% position:50% size:90%
In this synthetic sample, the all-users
headline shows one hundred and fifty-one

76
00:04:20.207 --> 00:04:23.893 line:76% position:50% size:90%
active users out of one hundred and
seventy roster users.

77
00:04:23.893 --> 00:04:26.674 line:76% position:50% size:90%
That is a demonstration of the
calculation,

78
00:04:26.674 --> 00:04:29.131 line:76% position:50% size:90%
not a claim about a real organisation.

79
00:04:29.131 --> 00:04:32.753 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Switch to Licensed to ask about matched
licence holders.

80
00:04:32.753 --> 00:04:37.797 line:76% position:50% size:90%
In the agent lens, the comparison is
people using agents against active people

81
00:04:37.797 --> 00:04:41.483 line:76% position:50% size:90%
using Copilot only, not a full inventory
of unused seats.

82
00:04:42.283 --> 00:04:47.164 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Use the organisation bars to find a team
that may need help getting started.

83
00:04:47.164 --> 00:04:51.274 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Then check access, training and the audit
window with that team.

84
00:04:51.274 --> 00:04:54.484 line:76% position:50% size:90%
If the licensed population does not
reconcile with

85
00:04:54.484 --> 00:04:57.502 line:76% position:50% size:90%
observed users, utilisation can be
unavailable.

86
00:04:57.502 --> 00:05:00.521 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Correct the identity and licence mapping
first.

87
00:05:00.521 --> 00:05:05.337 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Unlicensed activity alone does not
establish a need for a paid licence, and

88
00:05:05.337 --> 00:05:08.483 line:76% position:50% size:90%
inactivity alone is not permission to
remove one.

89
00:05:09.283 --> 00:05:13.192 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Usage moves from who is active to how
people use the tools.

90
00:05:13.192 --> 00:05:16.439 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Read active users and observed sessions
together.

91
00:05:16.439 --> 00:05:19.155 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A session is an observed conversation per

92
00:05:19.155 --> 00:05:22.070 line:76% position:50% size:90%
user and app, identified through its
thread.

93
00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:24.257 line:76% position:50% size:90%
It is not the same as an AI task.

94
00:05:24.257 --> 00:05:27.635 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The weekly chart compares populations on
one scale.

95
00:05:27.635 --> 00:05:32.803 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Sessions per user per week averages the
weekly rates over active weeks, rather

96
00:05:32.803 --> 00:05:35.983 line:76% position:50% size:90%
than dividing everything by every calendar
week.

97
00:05:36.783 --> 00:05:39.263 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Scroll down to the behaviour bars, the

98
00:05:39.263 --> 00:05:42.330 line:76% position:50% size:90%
organisation comparison and conversation
depth.

99
00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:45.919 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The depth chart groups sessions by prompts
per session.

100
00:05:45.919 --> 00:05:50.878 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A longer conversation may mean useful
iteration, a difficult task or trouble

101
00:05:50.878 --> 00:05:52.314 line:76% position:50% size:90%
getting a good answer.

102
00:05:52.314 --> 00:05:54.337 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The page cannot tell you which.

103
00:05:54.337 --> 00:05:57.143 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Estimated hours use the model’s time
rules;

104
00:05:57.143 --> 00:05:59.883 line:76% position:50% size:90%
they are not time recorded by a stopwatch.

105
00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:05.804 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Choose one population and one team, then
ask what work is behind its pattern.

106
00:06:05.804 --> 00:06:10.311 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A rising line is a reason to talk to
people, not a result by itself.

107
00:06:10.311 --> 00:06:15.812 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A practical next step is to find a useful
workflow, check the quality of its output

108
00:06:15.812 --> 00:06:18.861 line:76% position:50% size:90%
and share a small example with a similar
team.

109
00:06:18.861 --> 00:06:23.700 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Compare frequency and depth after that
change, using a comparable period.

110
00:06:24.533 --> 00:06:27.950 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The Heatmap makes changes over time easier
to spot.

111
00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:31.568 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Each row is an organisation and each
column is a week.

112
00:06:31.568 --> 00:06:34.717 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Darker cells mean more of the selected
measure.

113
00:06:34.717 --> 00:06:38.067 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The picker changes both the grid and the
headline.

114
00:06:38.067 --> 00:06:41.283 line:76% position:50% size:90%
You can show active users, active days per
user,

115
00:06:41.283 --> 00:06:44.298 line:76% position:50% size:90%
modelled hours per user or sessions per
user.

116
00:06:44.298 --> 00:06:47.983 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Always read that label before interpreting
a dark cell.

117
00:06:48.783 --> 00:06:50.836 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Active users tells you about reach.

118
00:06:50.836 --> 00:06:54.940 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A large team can lead on that measure just
because it has more people.

119
00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:58.048 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Per-user measures help you look at
intensity instead.

120
00:06:58.048 --> 00:07:01.097 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Modelled hours still depend on the time
assumptions.

121
00:07:01.097 --> 00:07:05.788 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A pale cell could reflect a quiet week,
incomplete collection or a short week at

122
00:07:05.788 --> 00:07:07.371 line:76% position:50% size:90%
the edge of the date range.

123
00:07:07.371 --> 00:07:09.833 line:76% position:50% size:90%
It does not explain the reason on its own.

124
00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:12.705 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Follow a row from left to right.

125
00:07:12.705 --> 00:07:16.714 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Look for a sustained change rather than
one unusually dark week.

126
00:07:16.714 --> 00:07:21.976 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Switch to sessions per user to see whether
a change in reach also changed intensity.

127
00:07:21.976 --> 00:07:26.236 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Then check the dates of a rollout,
training event or collection gap.

128
00:07:26.236 --> 00:07:30.747 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Use the pattern to choose where to
investigate, not to label one team as

129
00:07:30.747 --> 00:07:32.000 line:76% position:50% size:90%
better than another.

130
00:07:32.867 --> 00:07:38.246 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The Leaderboard ranks organisations and
people, alongside apps and behaviours.

131
00:07:38.246 --> 00:07:40.936 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Start with sessions and users together.

132
00:07:40.936 --> 00:07:44.798 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Expand an organisation to inspect the
people beneath it.

133
00:07:44.798 --> 00:07:47.971 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The app table can also expand into
behaviours.

134
00:07:47.971 --> 00:07:53.075 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Sorting a column changes the question:
total sessions finds scale, while a

135
00:07:53.075 --> 00:07:55.351 line:76% position:50% size:90%
per-user measure finds intensity.

136
00:07:55.351 --> 00:08:00.317 line:76% position:50% size:90%
These rankings describe the selected
evidence, not employee performance.

137
00:08:01.117 --> 00:08:05.297 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A team can have many sessions because it
has many active people.

138
00:08:05.297 --> 00:08:07.583 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A smaller team can lead on sessions

139
00:08:07.583 --> 00:08:09.347 line:76% position:50% size:90%
per user. Both can be true.

140
00:08:09.347 --> 00:08:12.417 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The population buttons let you compare
licensed

141
00:08:12.417 --> 00:08:15.160 line:76% position:50% size:90%
users, unlicensed users, agents or Cowork.

142
00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:19.994 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Check the current population before
comparing the table with another view.

143
00:08:19.994 --> 00:08:23.717 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Security Copilot is excluded here, as
stated on the page.

144
00:08:24.533 --> 00:08:28.837 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Use the list to find someone who may have
a useful practice to share.

145
00:08:28.837 --> 00:08:31.581 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Ask what they did and whether the result
was

146
00:08:31.581 --> 00:08:34.325 line:76% position:50% size:90%
useful, rather than rewarding a large
count.

147
00:08:34.325 --> 00:08:36.819 line:76% position:50% size:90%
In the agent lens, inspect the agent and

148
00:08:36.819 --> 00:08:39.626 line:76% position:50% size:90%
creator evidence before promoting an
example.

149
00:08:39.626 --> 00:08:41.684 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Keep the conversation supportive.

150
00:08:41.684 --> 00:08:44.553 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A ranking based on audit activity should
never

151
00:08:44.553 --> 00:08:47.733 line:76% position:50% size:90%
substitute for a fair assessment of
someone’s work.

152
00:08:48.533 --> 00:08:51.636 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Habits groups people by active days in a
month.

153
00:08:51.636 --> 00:08:53.748 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Beginner means one to five days.

154
00:08:53.748 --> 00:08:55.597 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Developing means six to ten.

155
00:08:55.597 --> 00:08:57.775 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Habitual means eleven to fifteen.

156
00:08:57.775 --> 00:08:59.623 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Power means sixteen or more.

157
00:08:59.623 --> 00:09:02.528 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The page uses the last complete month in
the

158
00:09:02.528 --> 00:09:05.895 line:76% position:50% size:90%
selected data, not automatically the
current month.

159
00:09:05.895 --> 00:09:10.383 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The label above the cards tells you which
month is being classified.

160
00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:14.995 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Read the monthly columns and the
organisation mix together.

161
00:09:14.995 --> 00:09:18.468 line:76% position:50% size:90%
They show whether usage is spreading
across more days.

162
00:09:18.468 --> 00:09:23.807 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Inactive is based on licensed roster users
with no activity, and is not measured in

163
00:09:23.807 --> 00:09:26.316 line:76% position:50% size:90%
the agent, Cowork or unlicensed slices.

164
00:09:26.316 --> 00:09:30.561 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The people table helps you inspect the
evidence behind the stages.

165
00:09:30.561 --> 00:09:33.070 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A stage is about frequency; it does not

166
00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:35.900 line:76% position:50% size:90%
measure expertise or the value of an
answer.

167
00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:39.780 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A good action is to choose a recurring
task with

168
00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:42.732 line:76% position:50% size:90%
a beginner group and offer practical
coaching.

169
00:09:42.732 --> 00:09:45.299 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Look again after another complete month.

170
00:09:45.299 --> 00:09:49.021 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Do not expect every role to use Copilot
every working day.

171
00:09:49.021 --> 00:09:53.513 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Compare similar jobs, account for leave
and check collection coverage.

172
00:09:53.513 --> 00:09:58.840 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The useful question is whether a relevant
workflow became easier and more reliable,

173
00:09:58.840 --> 00:10:01.150 line:76% position:50% size:90%
not whether everybody reached Power.

174
00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:06.336 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Agents combines a registry snapshot with
matched audit sessions.

175
00:10:06.336 --> 00:10:11.544 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The top cards distinguish registry
records, agents built in the organisation

176
00:10:11.544 --> 00:10:13.874 line:76% position:50% size:90%
and agents with observed activity.

177
00:10:13.874 --> 00:10:19.288 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The estate table adds users, sessions,
return rate, lifecycle and usage review.

178
00:10:19.288 --> 00:10:21.755 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Select an agent to open its details.

179
00:10:21.755 --> 00:10:24.496 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A registry listing says an agent exists.

180
00:10:24.496 --> 00:10:28.950 line:76% position:50% size:90%
An audit match says there is evidence of
use in the loaded dates.

181
00:10:29.783 --> 00:10:34.587 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The synthetic sample has one hundred and
four registry records, with activity

182
00:10:34.587 --> 00:10:36.021 line:76% position:50% size:90%
matched to forty-three.

183
00:10:36.021 --> 00:10:38.829 line:76% position:50% size:90%
That gap is deliberate evidence to
interpret,

184
00:10:38.829 --> 00:10:41.449 line:76% position:50% size:90%
not proof that the rest should be deleted.

185
00:10:41.449 --> 00:10:46.501 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The sample registry lacks Total Users, so
lifecycle and usage review fall back to

186
00:10:46.501 --> 00:10:48.498 line:76% position:50% size:90%
users observed in the audit log.

187
00:10:48.498 --> 00:10:51.804 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Registry freshness and the audit end date
can differ.

188
00:10:51.804 --> 00:10:55.983 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Cooling and dormancy are measured against
the audit end, not today.

189
00:10:56.783 --> 00:11:01.407 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Return rate is the share of observed users
with at least two sessions.

190
00:11:01.407 --> 00:11:04.181 line:76% position:50% size:90%
It indicates repeat use, not satisfaction.

191
00:11:04.181 --> 00:11:08.673 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Lifecycle labels and usage-review labels
answer different questions.

192
00:11:08.673 --> 00:11:14.090 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Start with an ownerless or cooling agent
and ask the owner to confirm its purpose,

193
00:11:14.090 --> 00:11:16.270 line:76% position:50% size:90%
audience and collection coverage.

194
00:11:16.270 --> 00:11:20.629 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Keep, improve or retire it through your
normal governance process.

195
00:11:20.629 --> 00:11:25.583 line:76% position:50% size:90%
This page is not Agent Monitor and it does
not measure runtime reliability.

196
00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:31.589 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Tasks brings together engagement, task
categories, models and specialist roles.

197
00:11:31.589 --> 00:11:36.207 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The summary cards show estimated hours,
value and value after licences.

198
00:11:36.207 --> 00:11:39.395 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Below them, the charts describe how people
engage

199
00:11:39.395 --> 00:11:42.387 line:76% position:50% size:90%
and which behaviours appear in the audit
data.

200
00:11:42.387 --> 00:11:45.834 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The classifications are assigned by
processing rules.

201
00:11:45.834 --> 00:11:51.168 line:76% position:50% size:90%
They help organise the evidence; they do
not prove that a document was accurate or

202
00:11:51.168 --> 00:11:53.250 line:76% position:50% size:90%
a business outcome was achieved.

203
00:11:54.117 --> 00:11:56.113 line:76% position:50% size:90%
An AI task here is a prompt row.

204
00:11:56.113 --> 00:11:59.108 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Distinct prompt messages are a separate
measure,

205
00:11:59.108 --> 00:12:01.791 line:76% position:50% size:90%
and resource-linked rows can affect
counts.

206
00:12:01.791 --> 00:12:05.160 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Read activity share as the share of those
prompt rows.

207
00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:10.151 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Shares of users reaching different roles
can overlap because the same person can

208
00:12:10.151 --> 00:12:11.711 line:76% position:50% size:90%
reach more than one role.

209
00:12:11.711 --> 00:12:15.767 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Do not add them up as though they were
mutually exclusive groups.

210
00:12:16.617 --> 00:12:20.826 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Scroll through the engagement, behaviours,
model and skills panels.

211
00:12:20.826 --> 00:12:23.779 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Choose one task group that matters to the
team.

212
00:12:23.779 --> 00:12:28.742 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Check that the inferred label fits the
actual workflow, then ask a small sample

213
00:12:28.742 --> 00:12:31.694 line:76% position:50% size:90%
of users about the quality and effort
involved.

214
00:12:31.694 --> 00:12:35.589 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The goal is to find where a deeper value
review is worthwhile.

215
00:12:35.589 --> 00:12:40.867 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A popular model or a broad skills list is
not evidence that every result was useful.

216
00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:46.518 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Estimated value shows expert-equivalent
hours and their money valuation.

217
00:12:46.518 --> 00:12:50.599 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Non-Cowork work uses behaviour-specific
human-time estimates.

218
00:12:50.599 --> 00:12:55.886 line:76% position:50% size:90%
For per-resource behaviours, the source
normalises resource rows using the mean

219
00:12:55.886 --> 00:13:00.101 line:76% position:50% size:90%
resources per prompt for that behaviour
across all loaded data.

220
00:13:00.101 --> 00:13:03.982 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Cowork uses task-category time bands and
read-chain rules.

221
00:13:03.982 --> 00:13:07.395 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Its run estimate is at least one, with
half-to-even

222
00:13:07.395 --> 00:13:10.406 line:76% position:50% size:90%
rounding where a read-chain estimate is
used.

223
00:13:10.406 --> 00:13:13.350 line:76% position:50% size:90%
It is not one fixed saving for every
prompt.

224
00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:19.538 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Hours per week divides estimated hours by
the selected window’s distinct weeks.

225
00:13:19.538 --> 00:13:23.187 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Money value is hours times hourly value
times penalty.

226
00:13:23.187 --> 00:13:28.728 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Annualised value scales the value using
the weeks present in the filtered rows and

227
00:13:28.728 --> 00:13:32.648 line:76% position:50% size:90%
fifty-two weeks; it is not a forecast of
realised savings.

228
00:13:32.648 --> 00:13:38.121 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Licence investment is the monthly price
times distinct licensed users observed in

229
00:13:38.121 --> 00:13:40.351 line:76% position:50% size:90%
each month, summed across months.

230
00:13:40.351 --> 00:13:44.000 line:76% position:50% size:90%
It is not your invoice or the entire
purchased estate.

231
00:13:44.867 --> 00:13:49.820 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Use the task-group table to see where
estimated hours come from, and expand a

232
00:13:49.820 --> 00:13:51.557 line:76% position:50% size:90%
group to inspect its tasks.

233
00:13:51.557 --> 00:13:55.096 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The lower tables show organisations and
users by agent.

234
00:13:55.096 --> 00:13:59.149 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The Tasks view contains the value and
licence-investment trend.

235
00:13:59.149 --> 00:14:04.424 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Value after licences subtracts modelled
investment; it is not a return percentage.

236
00:14:04.424 --> 00:14:09.249 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Compare conservative and optimistic
assumptions, then validate an important

237
00:14:09.249 --> 00:14:14.267 line:76% position:50% size:90%
workflow with users and real outcome
evidence before making a financial claim.

238
00:14:15.117 --> 00:14:18.496 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Signal map is the reference behind the
value pages.

239
00:14:18.496 --> 00:14:23.996 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The behaviour map links an audit signal to
a behaviour, use case and value outcome.

240
00:14:23.996 --> 00:14:27.110 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The human-time table shows the assumed
minutes,

241
00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:30.158 line:76% position:50% size:90%
categories, confidence and source
information.

242
00:14:30.158 --> 00:14:33.604 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Unlike the analysis views, it has no
cohort filters.

243
00:14:33.604 --> 00:14:36.586 line:76% position:50% size:90%
These are reference rules, not a separate
set

244
00:14:36.586 --> 00:14:39.767 line:76% position:50% size:90%
of activity totals for the current
organisation.

245
00:14:40.617 --> 00:14:43.849 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Search for a behaviour you saw in
Estimated value.

246
00:14:43.849 --> 00:14:48.827 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Check the low, typical and high time
estimates and the grain of the estimate.

247
00:14:48.827 --> 00:14:52.254 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Per task and per resource do not mean the
same thing.

248
00:14:52.254 --> 00:14:57.684 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Confidence and a cited source help explain
the assumption; they do not make a saving

249
00:14:57.684 --> 00:15:00.206 line:76% position:50% size:90%
directly observed in your organisation.

250
00:15:00.206 --> 00:15:04.667 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Cowork also has separate category and
chain logic in the calculation.

251
00:15:05.533 --> 00:15:09.344 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Use this page when someone asks where a
value number came from.

252
00:15:09.344 --> 00:15:11.583 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Trace the behaviour to the time rule,

253
00:15:11.583 --> 00:15:14.244 line:76% position:50% size:90%
record the scenario, and explain the
limits.

254
00:15:14.244 --> 00:15:19.144 line:76% position:50% size:90%
If the task label or assumed effort does
not match your workflow, investigate the

255
00:15:19.144 --> 00:15:22.653 line:76% position:50% size:90%
processing and methodology before relying
on the headline.

256
00:15:22.653 --> 00:15:27.613 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A transparent, cautious estimate is more
useful than a precise-looking number with

257
00:15:27.613 --> 00:15:28.883 line:76% position:50% size:90%
an unexplained basis.

258
00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:34.385 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Cowork fit grades the shape of the work
using recorded session evidence.

259
00:15:34.385 --> 00:15:39.525 line:76% position:50% size:90%
It looks at things such as cross-app
chains, building an artifact and sourcing.

260
00:15:39.525 --> 00:15:43.689 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Strong fit, Fair fit and Worth a look are
rule-based categories.

261
00:15:43.689 --> 00:15:46.097 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Some sessions are too light to grade.

262
00:15:46.097 --> 00:15:49.675 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The page cannot read answer quality from
those signals.

263
00:15:49.675 --> 00:15:54.100 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Treat a flag as a coaching prompt rather
than a verdict on a person.

264
00:15:54.950 --> 00:15:57.530 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Read What, Why and Who in that order.

265
00:15:57.530 --> 00:16:00.040 line:76% position:50% size:90%
What shows the mix by task category.

266
00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:03.108 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Select a category to narrow the
explanation.

267
00:16:03.108 --> 00:16:07.432 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Why shows the work shape, basis and
possible alternative tool.

268
00:16:07.432 --> 00:16:11.894 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Who shows the mix for groups with at least
five graded sessions.

269
00:16:11.894 --> 00:16:14.962 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Strong fit share uses graded sessions as
its

270
00:16:14.962 --> 00:16:18.240 line:76% position:50% size:90%
denominator, leaving unclassified sessions
out.

271
00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:20.750 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Changing grading can change the mix.

272
00:16:21.617 --> 00:16:24.853 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Imagine a fictional set of one hundred
sessions.

273
00:16:24.853 --> 00:16:28.561 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Eighty are graded, including forty Strong
fit sessions.

274
00:16:28.561 --> 00:16:30.314 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Twenty lack enough detail.

275
00:16:30.314 --> 00:16:32.742 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Strong fit share is forty divided by

276
00:16:32.742 --> 00:16:35.708 line:76% position:50% size:90%
eighty, or fifty percent, not forty
percent.

277
00:16:35.708 --> 00:16:39.282 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Next, inspect a Worth a look workflow with
its users.

278
00:16:39.282 --> 00:16:42.518 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Test whether a simpler tool would meet the
need.

279
00:16:42.518 --> 00:16:45.350 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A suggested alternative is a reason to try

280
00:16:45.350 --> 00:16:48.317 line:76% position:50% size:90%
a controlled comparison, not proof of
waste.

281
00:16:49.117 --> 00:16:53.380 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Model fit compares the weight of the work
with the logged model tier.

282
00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:56.160 line:76% position:50% size:90%
It uses the same grading rules as Cowork
fit.

283
00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:58.755 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A session is judged only when a recognised

284
00:16:58.755 --> 00:17:01.597 line:76% position:50% size:90%
model tier is logged and a grade is
available.

285
00:17:01.597 --> 00:17:04.624 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The coverage note matters as much as the
verdict.

286
00:17:04.624 --> 00:17:07.589 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Unattributed activity is not automatically
a bad

287
00:17:07.589 --> 00:17:10.617 line:76% position:50% size:90%
match; it is missing evidence for this
judgement.

288
00:17:11.450 --> 00:17:16.974 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A frontier-tier model on Worth a look work
can be flagged as Lighter model may do.

289
00:17:16.974 --> 00:17:21.689 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A workhorse-tier model on Strong fit work
can be flagged Try stronger.

290
00:17:21.689 --> 00:17:26.876 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The largest of good match, lighter and
stronger determines the group verdict.

291
00:17:26.876 --> 00:17:30.446 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Fewer than five judged sessions gives Too
few judged.

292
00:17:30.446 --> 00:17:34.017 line:76% position:50% size:90%
This is a rule-based prompt to test, not a
benchmark,

293
00:17:34.017 --> 00:17:37.250 line:76% position:50% size:90%
a measured quality score or a calculated
saving.

294
00:17:38.117 --> 00:17:41.976 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Inspect the model mix and the organisation
or person rows.

295
00:17:41.976 --> 00:17:46.169 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Check both the number of sessions and the
attribution coverage.

296
00:17:46.169 --> 00:17:51.226 line:76% position:50% size:90%
If a team is flagged, compare the same
representative task using appropriate

297
00:17:51.226 --> 00:17:55.418 line:76% position:50% size:90%
models, with quality, latency and any cost
measured separately.

298
00:17:55.418 --> 00:17:59.610 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Do not downgrade complex work simply to
make the chart greener.

299
00:17:59.610 --> 00:18:02.272 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Incomplete model logging is a collection

300
00:18:02.272 --> 00:18:05.267 line:76% position:50% size:90%
issue to fix, not a user behaviour to
punish.

301
00:18:06.117 --> 00:18:11.580 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Licences brings two questions together:
who might benefit from a licence next, and

302
00:18:11.580 --> 00:18:14.111 line:76% position:50% size:90%
which existing seats deserve a review?

303
00:18:14.111 --> 00:18:16.910 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The upper section ranks unlicensed demand.

304
00:18:16.910 --> 00:18:21.973 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Individual priority combines task
frequency and active days per active week.

305
00:18:21.973 --> 00:18:25.038 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Sixty points come from tasks, capped at
thirty

306
00:18:25.038 --> 00:18:27.969 line:76% position:50% size:90%
a week, and forty from days, capped at
five.

307
00:18:27.969 --> 00:18:31.367 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Group scores use medians before applying
that rule.

308
00:18:32.200 --> 00:18:35.139 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The lower section shows dormancy and
potential

309
00:18:35.139 --> 00:18:37.759 line:76% position:50% size:90%
reclaim value when the roster reconciles.

310
00:18:37.759 --> 00:18:42.233 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Days since activity are measured against
the latest loaded audit date.

311
00:18:42.233 --> 00:18:45.939 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A short collection window cannot prove
long-term dormancy.

312
00:18:45.939 --> 00:18:50.732 line:76% position:50% size:90%
In this sample, the audit span is
ninety-one days, so it cannot establish a

313
00:18:50.732 --> 00:18:53.288 line:76% position:50% size:90%
full one-hundred-and-eighty-day history.

314
00:18:53.288 --> 00:18:56.483 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Never active means no activity found in
the loaded

315
00:18:56.483 --> 00:18:59.550 line:76% position:50% size:90%
evidence, not never active in the person’s
life.

316
00:19:00.367 --> 00:19:04.683 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Read the evidence and costing notes before
using a reclaim estimate.

317
00:19:04.683 --> 00:19:08.683 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The licence price comes from Assumptions,
not a billing system.

318
00:19:08.683 --> 00:19:13.698 line:76% position:50% size:90%
First ask a high-priority candidate about
tasks, eligibility and whether a paid

319
00:19:13.698 --> 00:19:15.412 line:76% position:50% size:90%
feature is actually needed.

320
00:19:15.412 --> 00:19:18.332 line:76% position:50% size:90%
For a dormant seat, check leave, role
changes,

321
00:19:18.332 --> 00:19:20.744 line:76% position:50% size:90%
identity matching and collection gaps.

322
00:19:20.744 --> 00:19:23.410 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Use the page to prepare a review list, not

323
00:19:23.410 --> 00:19:26.267 line:76% position:50% size:90%
to purchase or revoke licences
automatically.

324
00:19:27.117 --> 00:19:31.498 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Cowork readiness looks for activity
patterns that may suit Cowork.

325
00:19:31.498 --> 00:19:37.008 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The cards describe eligible users, prompt
depth, apps per active day and agent use.

326
00:19:37.008 --> 00:19:42.319 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Eligibility in the summary means active
prompt users who have no Cowork activity

327
00:19:42.319 --> 00:19:44.377 line:76% position:50% size:90%
anywhere in the loaded dataset.

328
00:19:44.377 --> 00:19:49.157 line:76% position:50% size:90%
It does not mean that a person has
permission, a licence, or a confirmed

329
00:19:49.157 --> 00:19:50.817 line:76% position:50% size:90%
business need for Cowork.

330
00:19:51.617 --> 00:19:56.394 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The candidate score weights app breadth at
forty-five points, prompt depth at

331
00:19:56.394 --> 00:19:58.628 line:76% position:50% size:90%
thirty-five and agent use at twenty.

332
00:19:58.628 --> 00:20:02.475 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Breadth and depth are normalised against
the candidate maxima.

333
00:20:02.475 --> 00:20:06.011 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The ranking can change when the comparison
group changes.

334
00:20:06.011 --> 00:20:10.541 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The page excludes people with Cowork
history anywhere in the loaded data.

335
00:20:10.541 --> 00:20:12.898 line:76% position:50% size:90%
People without enough breadth or depth

336
00:20:12.898 --> 00:20:15.566 line:76% position:50% size:90%
evidence may not receive a candidate
score.

337
00:20:15.566 --> 00:20:18.917 line:76% position:50% size:90%
This is a relative ranking, not an
external benchmark.

338
00:20:19.783 --> 00:20:22.584 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Use the scatter plot to find an
organisation

339
00:20:22.584 --> 00:20:25.130 line:76% position:50% size:90%
with multi-app work and deeper sessions.

340
00:20:25.130 --> 00:20:27.994 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Expand the candidate table to inspect
people.

341
00:20:27.994 --> 00:20:30.794 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Then ask about an actual cross-app task,
the

342
00:20:30.794 --> 00:20:33.531 line:76% position:50% size:90%
sources involved and the required
controls.

343
00:20:33.531 --> 00:20:35.886 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Choose a small pilot, agree a quality

344
00:20:35.886 --> 00:20:38.687 line:76% position:50% size:90%
measure and compare effort before and
after.

345
00:20:38.687 --> 00:20:43.778 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Readiness is an invitation to investigate
fit, not a claim that Cowork will help

346
00:20:43.778 --> 00:20:45.433 line:76% position:50% size:90%
every high-scoring person.

347
00:20:46.283 --> 00:20:51.009 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Feedback shows reactions and comments from
the loaded product-feedback file.

348
00:20:51.009 --> 00:20:54.615 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The monthly charts show volume and
satisfaction over time.

349
00:20:54.615 --> 00:20:59.776 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Check their dates: with no date filters,
feedback can extend beyond the interaction

350
00:20:59.776 --> 00:21:02.326 line:76% position:50% size:90%
dates in the header, as this sample does.

351
00:21:02.326 --> 00:21:04.937 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The reaction picker narrows the selection.

352
00:21:04.937 --> 00:21:09.912 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Satisfaction is thumbs-up items divided by
all selected feedback items, not just

353
00:21:09.912 --> 00:21:13.394 line:76% position:50% size:90%
thumbs-up plus thumbs-down when other
types are present.

354
00:21:13.394 --> 00:21:15.383 line:76% position:50% size:90%
It is not a survey of all users.

355
00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:19.888 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Scroll to satisfaction by app, comment
words and themes.

356
00:21:19.888 --> 00:21:23.575 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A high percentage based on a few items is
weak evidence.

357
00:21:23.575 --> 00:21:28.843 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Word frequency is not sentiment: a word
may appear in both praise and criticism.

358
00:21:28.843 --> 00:21:31.807 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Some themes are excluded by processing
rules.

359
00:21:31.807 --> 00:21:37.272 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Comments can contain sensitive material in
your own export, so handle them with the

360
00:21:37.272 --> 00:21:39.182 line:76% position:50% size:90%
same care as other user data.

361
00:21:39.182 --> 00:21:41.750 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The sample comments here are fictional.

362
00:21:42.617 --> 00:21:46.935 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Look for a repeated topic with enough
feedback to justify attention.

363
00:21:46.935 --> 00:21:51.760 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Check whether it concerns access, answer
quality, task fit or another issue.

364
00:21:51.760 --> 00:21:56.586 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Talk to the relevant team, make a focused
improvement and look again at both

365
00:21:56.586 --> 00:21:59.190 line:76% position:50% size:90%
feedback volume and the mix of reactions.

366
00:21:59.190 --> 00:22:04.397 line:76% position:50% size:90%
If you did not load a feedback file, an
empty page is missing evidence, not a zero

367
00:22:04.397 --> 00:22:05.667 line:76% position:50% size:90%
satisfaction result.

368
00:22:06.533 --> 00:22:09.231 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The Guide explains how the app maps to the

369
00:22:09.231 --> 00:22:12.378 line:76% position:50% size:90%
report and provides a searchable metric
glossary.

370
00:22:12.378 --> 00:22:16.618 line:76% position:50% size:90%
It groups the report into seven data tabs
plus this reference tab.

371
00:22:16.618 --> 00:22:19.379 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Charts sometimes change shape: for
example,

372
00:22:19.379 --> 00:22:22.141 line:76% position:50% size:90%
ranked bars replace some pies and
treemaps.

373
00:22:22.141 --> 00:22:27.280 line:76% position:50% size:90%
That is a layout difference, not a reason
to reinterpret the underlying measure.

374
00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:31.583 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The app is the browser CSV dashboard, not
the separate DataHub App.

375
00:22:32.450 --> 00:22:37.073 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Search the glossary for a metric such as
return rate or estimated value.

376
00:22:37.073 --> 00:22:41.889 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The small information buttons elsewhere in
the app also expose definitions.

377
00:22:41.889 --> 00:22:44.072 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Read the local note and the actual

378
00:22:44.072 --> 00:22:46.898 line:76% position:50% size:90%
calculation when a denominator is
important.

379
00:22:46.898 --> 00:22:51.200 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The Guide describes the agent behaviour
privacy floor of two users.

380
00:22:51.200 --> 00:22:54.475 line:76% position:50% size:90%
That limited suppression does not provide
row-level

381
00:22:54.475 --> 00:22:57.300 line:76% position:50% size:90%
security or make an entire export
anonymous.

382
00:22:58.117 --> 00:23:01.021 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Bring the pages together in a simple loop.

383
00:23:01.021 --> 00:23:06.623 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Use Activation to find reach, Usage and
Habits to understand patterns, and Agents

384
00:23:06.623 --> 00:23:08.076 line:76% position:50% size:90%
to review the estate.

385
00:23:08.076 --> 00:23:13.332 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Use Tasks and Estimated value to focus an
investigation, then Signal map and

386
00:23:13.332 --> 00:23:15.130 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Assumptions to explain it.

387
00:23:15.130 --> 00:23:17.758 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Fit and Readiness suggest experiments.

388
00:23:17.758 --> 00:23:20.593 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Feedback tests another side of the story.

389
00:23:20.593 --> 00:23:25.988 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Record the dates, evidence limits and
assumptions, and validate a real benefit

390
00:23:25.988 --> 00:23:27.717 line:76% position:50% size:90%
before making a decision.
